  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWarmer and drier conditions have begun to develop across the\nWest Slope today, as weak higher pressure invades. Temperatures\nwill inch up toward average values by week's end. The dry\nconditions will help with flood waters that have begun to recede\nacross southern CO and southeast UT. A weak clipper system\nworking across the PACNW on Tuesday will bring a few clouds to\nnorthwest CO. Non zero precipitation chances could bring an\nisolated sprinkle or flurry to the high country up there, but\nlittle to no measurable moisture is expected.\n\nLate Thursday, models begin to develop a system that drops\nsouthward down the PACNW coast. Presently, this system digs\nsouthward Friday and Saturday. This will ramp up southwesterly\nflow across the Four Corners. Winds will likely pick up in the\nforecasts as the week progresses and models get a better handle\non this system. Storm tracks and depth of cold air are an early\npoint of concern in this forecast. Model runs have already\npushed impacts further out than initial runs were projecting.\nProbabilistic guidance is still indicating 50/50 chances for\nheavier rainfall across southwest CO Sunday and Monday. \nSaturated soils from this weekend's storms make this a much more\nimpactful forecast, as low end rainfall totals could push \nstreams back towards action stage. For now, the strongest \nmoisture push arrives Sunday into Monday. Snow levels remain \naround 10000 feet, which is very similar to the last storm, \nleaving the bulk of lower elevations in rainfall. This is a \ndeveloping forecast and will become more refined as details \nemerge from the models, giving more clarity.",
